# Styling: Tailwind CSS 4 + daisyUI 5 + Inspira UI

Docs: https://tailwindcss.com/docs/theme , https://daisyui.com/docs/config/ , https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/styling/ , https://inspira-ui.com/docs/en/getting-started/installation

## One stylesheet, CSS-first configuration

- `src/styles/global.css` is the only global stylesheet and is imported once in `BaseLayout.astro`. It holds `@import 'tailwindcss'`, `@import 'tw-animate-css'`, the daisyUI `@plugin` block, the Inspira backgrounds plugin, `@theme` tokens, and `@layer base` resets.
- There is no `tailwind.config.js`. Configure Tailwind in CSS: `@theme` for tokens that should become utilities, `@utility` for custom utilities, `@layer components` only for patterns repeated in many places that daisyUI does not cover.
- Add a design token to `@theme` once; never hard-code brand hex values in markup.

## daisyUI usage

- Use daisyUI components for foundations: `btn`, `card`, `badge`, `navbar`, `footer`, `drawer`, `collapse`, `modal`, `input`, `textarea`, `select`, `checkbox`, `alert`, `breadcrumbs`, `steps`, `tabs`. For rendered Markdown, write article styles in an Astro component or add `@tailwindcss/typography` (`prose`) only when long-form article styling is built; it is not installed yet, so record the decision in `docs/architecture.md`.
- Use daisyUI semantic colors (`bg-base-100`, `text-base-content`, `bg-primary`, `text-primary-content`, `border-base-300`) rather than raw palette colors, so every color comes from the theme.
- Light only (owner's decision, 2026-11-17): the site renders in daisyUI's `light` theme whatever the visitor's device or browser prefers. Do not add a dark theme, `--prefersdark`, `prefers-color-scheme` queries, `dark:` classes, or a theme toggle without a new decision; the smoke suite fails if the page changes under a dark preference.
- How light-only holds, all four together: daisyUI loads only `light --default`; `BaseLayout.astro` sets `data-theme="light"` on `<html>` and `<meta name="color-scheme" content="only light">`; `global.css` sets `color-scheme: only light` on `:root`, unlayered so it beats the plain `light` daisyUI's theme sets in the base layer (with plain `light`, Chromium's forced dark mode, Chrome's "Darken websites", still recolours the page); and Tailwind's `dark:` variant is bound to `[data-theme=dark]`, which nothing sets.
- Primary calls to action render through `src/components/ui/Button.astro`: a pill-shaped `btn btn-primary` with the lit finish from the `btn-lit` utility in `global.css` (gradient, four shadows, flipped on press). Apply `btn-lit` by hand only where an Astro component cannot be used, such as inside a Vue island. `variant="secondary"` gives the next-best action beside a primary one: `btn-outline btn-secondary`, transparent with the owner's near-black label and 1px border, which fills near-black with a white label under the pointer, with no shadow in any state. `variant="neutral"` is for a call to action on the primary yellow, where a primary would not stand out (the owner's mockup, 2026-11-19, for the home directory's "Let's talk"): daisyUI's `btn-neutral`, a flat near-black fill with a light label and the same pill (`rounded-full`) as the other buttons, with no shadow, glow, text shadow, noise texture (`bg-none`), or shift on press. On light backgrounds give these buttons a near-black focus ring (`focus-visible:outline-neutral`): daisyUI's ring takes the button colour, and the yellow is 1.4:1 there.
- daisyUI's `aura` (border light) always carries one of the two motion utilities in `global.css`; a bare `aura` circles forever and breaks the five-second motion limit. `aura-lap` (`Button.astro`'s `aura`) makes one lap on appearance, then lights only on hover or keyboard focus; let at most one lap play per view. `aura-hover` (`aura="hover"`, every primary except the navbar, and the three cards of the home "Why Auralink Studio?", whose list items are the wrappers, in `primary` so the light is yellow on the dark band: the owner's choice of 2026-11-20 over a fill) stays unlit until hover or keyboard focus, then fades in and circles; leaving fades it out over the same 0.4s, and the paused `aura` animation keeps its place so the next visit continues from there. Its wrapper takes no room, so the button renders exactly as it does without the light. The pause keeps the button on its own layer, because daisyUI's keyframes set a transform; that is what lets the light resume. The light takes the surrounding text colour, so set a `text-*` colour on the parent when the surface is light. Both utilities fade the light through `--aura-light`, its strength in the gradient, never through `color`: Safari without WebKit's fix of 2026-10-09 does not repaint a gradient whose only change is currentColor, so the light stayed on. The aura raises the button it wraps above static siblings: make neighbours its glow reaches `relative` (the hero row's `*:relative`) so they still paint above it.
- A colour that fades on hover or focus fades through registered custom properties (`@property`), not through `background-color` or `color`, and a part that slides moves through one too, not through `translate` itself: WebKit styles the page once before the stylesheet arrives, and a transition on the property itself then starts on load, from the browser's defaults. The model is the `arrow-box` utility in `global.css`, the arrow at the foot of each home service card. It began as the arrow of the service cards on Johnny's Exclusive Barbershop's website, the owner's work, which the owner asked for (2026-11-18), and keeps its place and shape: a 50px box with 0.25rem corners. Its look and hover are this site's own, made when the owner asked for a modern hover (2026-11-19) and awaiting the owner's confirmation: white with a hairline edge, a small shadow (`shadow-md`), and a near-black arrow. Under `arrow-box-active` (its card's `group-hover:` and `group-focus-visible:`) the box fills primary over 0.3s (`--arrow-box-fill`), in step with the card's frame, while the arrow slides out to the right and the same arrow slides in from the left over 0.4s (`--arrow-box-travel`), both on the frame's curve. The arrow is near-black (`primary-content`) in every state, 19.9:1 on the white and 14.3:1 on the yellow, and never changes colour: do not fade it. An earlier fade from primary to near-black passed through dark olive and left a dark mark after the frame and the fill had settled, and the barbershop's own swap of primary for white was 1.4:1 in both states. Only the two arrows move, inside the box, which clips them: the box and the card never move or resize. The lead card, Custom Websites, uses `arrow-box-lead`: the box starts primary and turns white, with the same arrow and slide, and no shadow, which would not show on the near-black tile. Inside a link, point only the background at a fading property. While a registered property is mid-transition, Chrome 157 and 158 paint a `border-color` or `color` that refers to it as if the declaration were invalid, on anything inside a visited link: the border in the text colour, the text in the inherited colour. The box's edge was first mixed from `--arrow-box-fill`, so once a card's destination had been visited it flashed near-black for the length of every fade, in and out (the owner's recording, 2026-11-19). The edge is now one translucent colour, the near-black at 9%, which the fill shows through. Computed styles do not show the fault and Playwright's Chromium does not have it, so check such a change in installed Chrome with the link visited (`docs/quality-checks.md`, manual checks). `tests/e2e/services.spec.ts` checks the colours, the slide, that every box has the one edge colour, and that no card starts a transition on load. The home directory's cells follow the same model for colour (`directory-cell`): white with charcoal text, then, only inside `@media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine)`, the secondary near-black (`#141817`) with a white name, icon, and 1px underline 5px under the label, over 240ms; `tests/e2e/industries.spec.ts` makes the same check. The footer's list links follow it too (`footer-link`): the footer's light text, primary under a pointer that can hover, over 200ms. They first used Tailwind's utility that transitions every colour property, `color` among them: WebKit faded them in on load from the browser's link blue, 2.1:1 on the footer's near-black, and the accessibility scan of the home page caught the labels on the way (mobile WebKit, 2026-11-19). `tests/e2e/footer.spec.ts` makes the same check. Their `color` is the fading property, so in Chrome 157 and 158 a visited footer link changes colour without the fade: it stays light for the 200ms and then turns primary, and turns back at once. Nothing dark shows, and it is left so until the owner decides (found 2026-11-19).
- Brand palette: only the owner decides brand colours. Decided so far: primary `#f7db15` (2026-11-17), the colour of every primary call to action, with the `neutral` near-black as its label (white on it is 1.4:1); secondary `#141817` (2026-11-17), the label and border of the secondary call to action and its fill under the pointer, where the label turns white. Each decided colour goes in the `@plugin 'daisyui/theme' { name: 'light'; default: true; ... }` block in `global.css`: daisyUI merges a block named after a built-in theme into that theme's tokens, so `data-theme="light"`, the smoke test, and the light-only setup stay as they are. Record each colour in the "Brand colours" row of `docs/architecture.md`; every colour not yet decided stays at daisyUI's `light` value.

## Markup rules

- Mobile-first: write base styles for small screens, then `md:` and `lg:` overrides.
- Page container: every section centres its content with `mx-auto max-w-site px-6 lg:px-8`, so edges line up from the navbar down. `max-w-site` is the `--container-site` token in `global.css` (88rem, owner, 2026-11-18). The hero headline's size cap and the `sizes` of the home images are worked out from it: change them together.
- No inline `style=""` except for truly dynamic values; no `!important` in markup.
- Keep class lists readable: layout, spacing, typography, color, state. Extract a component when the same combination appears three times.
- Spacing and sizing come from the Tailwind scale; arbitrary values (`w-[437px]`) need a comment explaining why.
- Focus styles must stay visible: never add `outline-none` without a replacement `focus-visible:` style.

## Inspira UI

- Inspira components are copied into `src/components/inspira/` from the official docs, keeping their file names, then adapted. They import `cn` from `@/lib/utils`. Installed support packages: `@vueuse/core`, `motion-v`, `tw-animate-css`, `@inspira-ui/plugins` (background utilities loaded via `src/styles/inspira-backgrounds.mjs`).
- Some components depend on additional pieces (shadcn-vue primitives, `reka-ui`, icons). Read the component's import list before copying; add a dependency only when the component is approved, and note it in `docs/architecture.md`.
- Use effects purposefully: at most one or two per page, on hero backgrounds, section dividers, or interactive cards. Never animate body text, navigation, or form controls. Every effect must respect `prefers-reduced-motion` (global CSS gives every animation and transition zero duration and delay, so nothing moves; also skip JS-driven loops when reduced motion is on).
- The home services strip is the exception to "never animate body text", and only that strip: `src/components/sections/ServiceStrip.astro` with the `service-strip` utility. Its labels, a bold first word and light remainder with an eight-point asterisk between names, in `primary-content` on a `primary` band with a `neutral` band tilted 2.2 degrees behind it (the owner's reference, 2026-11-18), scroll slowly and continuously, run off both edges with no fade, and hold still while the pointer is over the band; there is no pause button (owner, 2026-11-18). The tilt's depth at the screen's edges, `--service-strip-rise` in the utility, is half the width times tan(2.2deg). The animation is not applied under reduced motion or without JavaScript; both show the same labels in a static wrap. Do not reuse that animation on other text.
- The home hero's benefit orbit is this site's own CSS, not an Inspira component: `src/components/ui/ServiceOrbit.astro` with the `orbit` utility (owner, 2026-11-18). Six chips from `heroBenefits` in `src/data/site.ts` sit on a ring behind the devices, each a Lucide outline icon with its benefit as the label, like an app icon (owner, 2026-11-18). More sales and Repeat customers are filled primary; the other four are white. The icons are decorative. The icons arrive once on load and their labels fade in as they stop (3.4s in all); the labels never travel, which keeps "never animate body text". After that the icons stay put: a label that moved would slide behind the laptop. Once the ring has opened a light circles it, as the `aura` circles the buttons, for as long as the page is open, pointer or not (owner, 2026-11-18): with the services strip, the only motion allowed past five seconds (`.cursor/rules/accessibility-performance.mdc`). Nothing moves under reduced motion. With the pencil underline, that is all the motion the hero starts by itself: do not add more there, and do not let anything else in the orbit run by itself past five seconds without the owner's decision. The ring fits the room given by `--orbit-above` and `--orbit-below`, which `Hero.astro` sets from the hero's gap and paddings. The chips' resting angle, their order around the ring, and where each label sits are tuned to the device image, so that no icon or label rests behind a device or off the screen. Change the paddings, the gap, the image, a label, or the number of benefits, and `tests/e2e/hero.spec.ts` says whether the placing still holds. The services strip below the hero still lists the services.
- The hero of every page except the home page is `src/components/sections/PageIntro.astro` with the `page-hero` utility (the owner's request, 2026-11-19): a `neutral` band straight under the navbar, a `primary` eyebrow that is the page's name in navigation (`eyebrow`, required, since 2026-11-20; the pricing page's is the brief's "Simple, transparent pricing", the one exception), a white `h1` whose `highlight` words are `primary`, a `primary` rule, the description, and an optional `note` under it, a short line with a small `primary` icon (the pricing page's two-week design preview). A page with a parent hub also passes `breadcrumbs` (Home and the hub linked, the page last and unlinked) and its `icon`, which render above the eyebrow: a wrapping breadcrumb landmark with 44px links, then the icon at 40px, stroke 1.5, in `primary`. The owner's mark (`src/assets/aura-hero.svg`, used as supplied) rests on the band's bottom edge at the right, on a `primary` glow and three rings which that edge cuts in half, beside the copy from `lg` and in a row of its own under the copy below `lg`. A new page gets this hero by rendering `PageIntro` first in `main`: pass `highlight` only with words that are in the title as written, and give the content after it its own top padding (`pt-16 lg:pt-20`), since the band has none to lend. The art arrives once on load, within 2.3s, and nothing moves after that or under reduced motion: do not add motion that runs on, and do not animate the copy. Every part of the art is a fraction of the disc (`--page-hero-disc`), so resize the art's column or row, not the parts. `tests/e2e/page-hero.spec.ts` says whether the mark still rests on its rings, clear of the copy and inside the screen, at 320-1920 px.
- The industry and service detail pages (the design pass of 2026-11-20, after uprig.net/seo, the owner's reference for their cards, in this site's colours; no copy changed) share one visual system, and the pricing page (2026-11-20, after uprig.net/pricing for its layout only) builds on it: `DetailSection`, `SectionHeading`, `detail-card`, the dark process band with a `note` callout under its steps, `DetailFaq` with eleven questions, and the mark across the process and questions bands, plus its own sections (`src/components/sections/Pricing*.astro`): the package cards, Business on `secondary` with a `primary` badge, the comparison table that re-flows its rows below md, the welcome series on a `primary` band, the Website Care card with a `neutral` head, and a closing call to action whose card is white so the primary and the secondary button both sit on it. Every section after the hero renders through `src/components/sections/DetailSection.astro`, which gives the band (`light` white, `muted` the home services' grey, `dark` the home process's charcoal with its `process-lines`) and the container's rhythm, and `src/components/ui/SectionHeading.astro` opens each with a short `primary` dash before the uppercase eyebrow, the h2 (48px from lg), and the intro; the dash replaces the rule the home sections draw under their headings, so a section opens with one accent. Cards use the `detail-card` utility in `global.css` (white, 1px `base-300`, `rounded-md`, `shadow-sm`: the contact page's card), with the component's own padding and layout: numbered deliverables with a large Mukta number and a `primary` full stop at the left; challenge cards in two halves, the problem in the dark with a `primary` number and the response on white; related service cards that are one link each, with the home cards' 2px `primary` frame (`before:-inset-px`, over the card's own edge) and `arrow-box`; journey step cards with a `primary` numbered disc and a hairline that crosses the gap to the next card in the row only (`md:-mr-11 lg:-mr-12`, the card's padding plus the grid's gap, and the list is not positioned so the next card covers the overshoot); path cards with a 4px `primary` top edge; comparison cards with a grey head; FAQ disclosures as cards whose plus on a bordered disc turns into a minus on a `primary` disc when open. Notes (a module's `note` block, a service page's scope note) are the same card with a 4px `primary` left edge, written out in full rather than combined with `detail-card`. Dark bands set numbers in `primary`; light cards set them near-black with the `primary` stop. The pixel before a list title (`size-1.5 rotate-45 bg-primary`) is the logo's own. Nothing on these pages moves on load, and no colour fades: hover and open states change at once, except the frame's opacity, which is a pseudo-element the stylesheet creates.
- The About page (2026-11-20, after uprig.net/about, the owner's reference, in this site's colours) is built from that same system and adds no utility: `AboutStory.astro` is the detail introduction's split (`SectionHeading`, the lead with its 4px `primary` left bar, two paragraphs) with the owner's portrait in the narrower column from lg, `rounded-md` on a `neutral` panel that is the photo's own box moved 20px down and right (`translate-x-5 translate-y-5`, inside the container's padding so it never passes the screen) and a 6px `primary` bar on the panel's lower lip at the photo's left, the reference's accent bar; `AboutPrinciples.astro` puts `whyPillars` on the dark band's cards (`DetailProcess`'s card) with a 4px `primary` top edge and a `primary` number, all three alike, with the heading left and the lead right from lg; `AboutServices.astro` is three `detail-card`s with the pathway cards' near-black icon tile, the catalogue services as underlined links opened by the logo's pixel, and `servicesCta` as a secondary `Button`. The mark lies between those two bands. Nothing on the page moves after the hero, and no colour fades.
- The section mark, `src/components/ui/SectionMark.astro` with the `section-mark` utility, lays the logo's mark across the edge between two sections, after the Eyler Creative page the owner gave as the reference (2026-11-20). Pass `mark="bottom"` to the section above and `mark="top"` to the section below (every `Detail*` section that renders through `DetailSection` takes it): each renders its own copy, flush right, centred on the shared edge, clipped to its own band, and tinted for it (`text-base-100/8` on dark, `text-primary/20` on light), so the halves read as one shape in two tints. It is 24vw wide between 11rem and 26rem, hidden below md, still, and inside the screen (the layout suite fails any box past the viewport, so it cannot bleed off the edge as the reference's does). A band with the mark on its bottom edge pads to `lg:pb-36` so the upper half meets no card; a band with it on its top edge must start with its heading at the left (every detail section does; the FAQ's disclosures start at `lg:pt-14` for the same reason). Placed once per page: on service pages between the process band and the questions, on industry pages between the challenges band and whatever follows it. Do not place it where either neighbour's content runs to the right edge next to the shared edge. The home "Why Auralink Studio?" band draws the same file another way (the owner's requests, 2026-11-20): faint, twice, from lg, with Tailwind classes alone (`text-primary/10`, `**:fill-current`, 24vw between 14rem and 25rem), behind the copy and the cards and inside the screen: whole in its own top right corner, and rising from its own bottom left corner with the lower half past the edge, which the band clips, so the band pads to `lg:pb-36` as a band with a section mark on its bottom edge does. Neither is a section mark (the next band does not continue the lower half), so they do not count against that placement.
- Effects must not shift layout or block interaction. Hydrate with `client:visible` or `client:idle`, and keep each effect's JavaScript small enough that Lighthouse performance stays above 90 on mobile.

## Scroll reveals and filters

- One mechanism, no animation library and no island: put `data-reveal` on the element whose arrival starts the entrance, give the parts that move a reveal utility from `global.css`, and call `revealOnScroll()` from `src/lib/reveal.ts` in the component's `<script>`. The utility is `pop-out`, after Hub2B's "Meet our hub space": an element tucked partly under a surface slides out by `--pop-x`/`--pop-y` over 1s with `ease`. First used for the props behind the home page's selected work cards (2026-11-18), which no page renders since that day; the two props behind the home directory of businesses (`WhoWeWorkWith.astro`) used it for part of 2026-11-20, each `picture` its own `data-reveal` element, until the owner had them removed, so no built page runs a reveal now. `revealOnScroll()` decides what is on screen only once the page has loaded with its stylesheet, as WebKit can run scripts before it applies.
- It plays once, the first time the element comes into view, after the images it displays have loaded. Nothing moves without JavaScript, under reduced motion, in print, or when the element is on screen at load. An element on screen at load turns `shown` only after the page has loaded and two frames have rendered: WebKit can style the page before the stylesheet applies, and an earlier `shown` turned the stylesheet's arrival into a transition. Move imagery only, never text, links, or controls.
- Never hide what is revealed with `clip-path` or `display`: a clipped ancestor counts as out of view for IntersectionObserver, so a lazy image under it never loads, and a lazy image under `display: none` never loads either. Tuck it behind a surface, or cover it.
- Filtering without a script: the `filter-group` utility shows the n-th panel of `[data-filter-panels]` when the n-th option of `[data-filter-options]` (a radio inside a label) is checked, up to six. Panels share one grid cell, so the height never jumps, and unchosen ones are invisible, out of the tab order and the accessibility tree, and set `--pop-open: 0` so their `pop-out` props slide out when chosen. Style the labels as pills with `has-checked:` and `has-focus-visible:`.
- `tests/e2e/work.spec.ts` shows how to test both: tucked below the fold, out once in view, no box moved, no `transitionrun` on load when on screen, never under reduced motion or without JavaScript; filters by click, arrow keys, and without JavaScript. It is skipped while no page renders selected work, so nothing exercises the reveal until a page uses it again; give that page a focused spec with the same checks.

## Fonts and icons

- Body text is Catamaran (`--font-main`). Headings are Mukta: `h1` and `h2` use `--font-headers`, and `h3`–`h6` use `--font-subheaders` (the same Mukta stack, so the two roles can diverge later). `--font-sans` points at `--font-main`. Both families are self-hosted through Astro's font support in `astro.config.mjs`, latin subset, `font-display: swap`, and `<Font>` in `BaseLayout` writes the variables. Do not add a third family or a font stylesheet from a CDN. Size a line that must stay unbroken for Mukta; the hero headline's comment in `src/components/sections/Hero.astro` records how its size was chosen.
- Icons: Lucide only (proposed 2026-11-18 for the home service cards, awaiting the owner's confirmation). Its SVG files are copied unchanged into `src/assets/icons/` (no icon package is installed), listed in `src/lib/icons.ts`, and drawn with `src/components/ui/Icon.astro`, which inlines them at build time: no JavaScript, icon font, or request. They are decorative (`aria-hidden`), so the text beside an icon carries its meaning; an icon-only control needs an `aria-label`. Add an icon from the same Lucide release and extend its entry in `src/assets/CREDITS.md`; never mix in another family or emoji. Two exceptions. The services strip's divider is an eight-point asterisk drawn in `ServiceStrip.astro`, the mark between the names in the owner's reference (2026-11-18); Lucide's asterisk is six strokes, so it is not that mark. `tooth.svg` in `src/assets/icons/`, for Dental in the home directory, is this site's own drawing on Lucide's 24px grid and stroke rules (2026-11-19), because Lucide has no tooth. Draw an icon only when Lucide has nothing for the subject, and mark the file as not Lucide's.

## Responsive QA

- Check every layout change at 360, 390, 768, 1024, 1440, and 1920 px with `npx playwright test tests/e2e/layout.spec.ts --project=desktop-chromium`, then inspect the screenshots it writes to `reports/screenshots/`. Procedure: `.cursor/skills/responsive-qa/SKILL.md`.
- Prevent overflow at the source: `min-w-0` on flex children, `break-words` for long strings, `max-w-*` containers, fluid images (`w-full h-auto` with explicit dimensions), responsive grids. Never hide overflow to mask a layout bug.
- Mobile projects in Playwright are emulations; physical-device checks are a separate, manual step before launch.

## Checks for this area

`npx prettier --check <files>`, `npm run build` (Tailwind errors surface at build), `npm run test:e2e` (axe catches contrast and focus regressions).
